
`native/` holds the AUnit tests for ImplementKipper/InterfaceKipper
(../lib/KipperbesturingCore/src/), run natively (no Teensy attached):

- `native/tests/` -- one `test_<ClassName>.cpp` per class under test.
- `native/support/` -- a small, self-contained set of Arduino-shaped stubs
  (Print/Stream/EEPROM/Arduino.h/etc.), not a real EpoxyDuino dependency, plus
  `native_main.cpp` (the host `int main()` entry point). No `VehicleGps.hpp`
  stub here -- GPS was dropped for this module (see "Known gaps" below).
  `Arduino.h` here also adds a `digitalWrite()`/`digitalWriteValue()`
  readback pair (mirroring the existing `analogWrite()`/`analogWriteValue()`
  pattern) -- `ImplementKipper::Adjust()` is the first module in this family
  to drive its bang-bang outputs with plain `digitalWrite()` rather than a
  variable-strength `analogWrite()`, since this module never had a
  manPwm/autoPwm split.
- `native/KipperbesturingNativeTests.cpp` -- the single AUnit driver
  (`setup()`/`loop()`), matching every other Triton module's `*NativeTests.cpp`
  pattern. Every `test_*.cpp` file only registers `test(...)` blocks; none of
  them define `setup()`/`loop()` themselves, so they all link into one
  combined binary instead of one binary per test file.
- `native/run_tests_msvc.ps1` -- MSVC fallback for Windows without GCC.

```
pio run -e native
.pio/build/native/program(.exe)
```

Needs a real host C++17 compiler on PATH (g++ or clang++) -- on Windows
without one, use test\native\run_tests_msvc.ps1 instead, which builds and
runs the same combined binary via MSVC directly. That script passes
`/Zc:preprocessor` -- MSVC's legacy preprocessor mishandles AUnit's
`test(SuiteName, testName)` argument-count dispatch, which every test in
this project uses.

## Known gaps

- Kipperbesturing's legacy source was closer to complete than most
  distilled modules -- a coherent P/I/D-to-shutoff-latch-bang-bang design,
  structurally a close cousin of ImplementPlanter's XTE control loop -- but
  the user confirmed it was never actually finished. Two real gaps closed
  here (see the distillation commit message for the full account):
  - **Hold mode is reconstructed, not ported.** The legacy source declared
    (and constructed) a `VehicleGps` solely so `updateScreen()`'s dead
    `case 1: // HOLD` branch could call `gps->minSpeed()`/`gps->getQuality()`
    -- but `update()`'s mode ladder never actually transitioned to Hold, and
    nothing in `ImplementKipper` ever used GPS for real. Dropped GPS
    entirely and implemented a real Hold transition using
    `VehicleTractor::MinSpeed()` (already real, already used by this
    module's own D-term) -- auto-steering now genuinely pauses while the
    tractor is stationary. Not validated against real hardware.
  - **The `TEENSY` board pin assignment in `ConfigKipper.hpp`/
    `ConfigImplementKipper.hpp`/`ConfigInterfaceKipper.hpp` is new** -- the
    legacy source only ever targeted a `VOORSERIE` (Arduino Uno/Leonardo)
    board. Pin numbers follow the same shape every sibling module's
    `TEENSY` branch uses, but haven't been validated against real hardware
    either.
- `CalibrationKipper` (the LCD/button calibration wizard) is `#ifdef
  ARDUINO`-guarded and deliberately excluded from `[env:native]`'s
  `build_src_filter` -- same reason as `CalibrationPlanter`/
  `CalibrationRooier`.
- `InterfaceKipper::UpdateScreen()` (LCD rendering) compiles as part of
  `InterfaceKipper.cpp` (needed since `CheckButtons()`/`Update()`, which
  *are* tested, live in the same file) but its output isn't asserted on by
  any test -- `test/native/support/InterfaceI2CLCD.hpp`'s `WriteBuffer`/
  `WriteScreen` are no-ops.
- The `teensy41` real-board env (in `src/main.cpp`) has not been
  build-verified on real hardware -- only via `pio run -e teensy41`'s
  cross-compile in this environment. Kipperbesturing also has no CAN/ISOBUS
  wiring at all -- it never used GPS/ISOBUS in the first place, and the
  angle/steer sensors are both purely local.
- Only the `teensy41` board is wired up today. This module's legacy source
  only ever targeted the `VOORSERIE` (Arduino Uno/Leonardo pre-series)
  board -- that `#ifdef` branch remains in `ConfigKipper.hpp`/
  `ConfigImplementKipper.hpp`/`ConfigInterfaceKipper.hpp` for future
  revival, but has no buildable `platformio.ini` env today. Unlike every
  other Triton module, this legacy source never had `TEENSY`/`TEENSYPROTO`/
  `MICRO` branches at all.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing (not used here -- pio test's
own Unity-based runner doesn't understand this project's AUnit setup):
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html
